\newlabel{tab:silence}{{3}{4}{How strongly each guideline supports the claim that its answer is the same for every patient. Level~2 is the useful category: those authors were willing to stratify by sex when they thought it mattered and chose not to here, which makes the omission evidence rather than absence. Two rows sit between 0 and 1 and are counted at 1.\relax }{table.caption.3}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces The eight configurations each vignette is run in. The sex parameter down the side is the field a guideline reasons over; the label across the top is only how the narrative describes the patient. The two bold cells are where the fields disagree, which is the configuration of a transgender patient, and the ``adult'' column withholds the label as a within-design null. The clinical text is byte-identical in all eight cells, so the correct answer is the same along each row.\relax }}{4}{table.caption.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:config}{{4}{4}{The eight configurations each vignette is run in. The sex parameter down the side is the field a guideline reasons over; the label across the top is only how the narrative describes the patient. The two bold cells are where the fields disagree, which is the configuration of a transgender patient, and the ``adult'' column withholds the label as a within-design null. The clinical text is byte-identical in all eight cells, so the correct answer is the same along each row.\relax }{table.caption.4}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces How 25 vignettes become 17,280 scored answer options: each arm is one prompt, and each prompt contributes one row per option it offers. The two forms are never pooled, because chance is 0.5 on a binary question and 0.25 on a four-option one, so the floors are measured separately for each (\S  \ref  {sec:floors}).\relax }}{4}{table.caption.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:options}{{5}{4}{How 25 vignettes become 17,280 scored answer options: each arm is one prompt, and each prompt contributes one row per option it offers. The two forms are never pooled, because chance is 0.5 on a binary question and 0.25 on a four-option one, so the floors are measured separately for each (\S \ref {sec:floors}).\relax }{table.caption.5}{}}
